{"n":10,"paragraphs":["Swamiji said that the urge to worship is part of every human being. Only the very highest philosophy can rise to pure abstraction, so people will always give God some form in order to worship. That is perfectly fine, he said, as long as the symbol is honoured as a pointer to the Divine behind it, not for its own sake. What we really need to escape is the superstition that something is true simply because 'it is in the books'. Forcing science, religion and philosophy all to fit what some book says is a terrible tyranny. Worshipping a book, he said, is the worst kind of idolatry.","Then he told this fable. There was once a stag, proud and free, who was showing off to his young one. Look at my strong horns, he said. One charge and I could kill a man. What a fine thing it is to be a stag!","Just then, far away, a hunter's bugle sounded. Instantly the stag bolted, with his puzzled little one racing behind. When they were finally safe, the young deer asked: Father, you are so strong and brave, so why do you run away from a man?","The stag admitted the truth. He knew he was strong and powerful. But the moment he heard that sound, something took hold of him and made him run, whether he wanted to or not.","We are just the same, said Swamiji. When we hear the 'bugle' of rules laid down in books, our habits and old superstitions grab hold of us. Before we know it we are tied up tight, and we forget our real nature, which is freedom."],"learn":"Habits and conditioning can make us react before we think, even when we know better. Notice your own 'bugle sounds', the triggers that send you running. Question rules you follow only because 'that's how it's done'.","source":"Vol. 8, pp. 33–34","images":["data:image/jpeg;base64,"]}